            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Divides a range of values into equal bins and counts how many of the input's values fall into each. The counts are the shape of the data: where it piles up, where it is sparse, whether it has one peak or two, how far it spreads.\n\nUse it to see what a sensor actually reports over time, to find the levels a signal sits at, to choose a threshold between two clusters, or as a feature in its own right - the distribution of speeds in a movement, of brightness in an image.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.histogram <bins: int> <min: float> <max: float>\nDefaults: 10 bins, auto range. Giving min and max turns auto range off.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.histogram 20 0 4\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe values: a list or array of any shape (every element is counted). nan and infinity belong in no bin and are left out. Every input is counted and sent.\n\nbins (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many bins.\n\nmin, max (float boxes, also inlets):\nThe range the bins cover, when auto range is off. Values outside it are not counted; a value exactly at max goes in the last bin.\n\nauto range:\nAn option, under the node. On, the bins span the input's own smallest to largest value, so they move with the data; off, they stay at min to max, so one count can be compared with the next.\n\ndensity:\nAn option, under the node. On, the counts are scaled so that the bars' total AREA is 1 - a probability density, comparable between inputs of different sizes.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\ncounts:\nOne count per bin.\n\nbin edges:\nWhere the bins start and end: one more value than there are bins. Sent before the counts.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: four random numbers, each anywhere from 0 to 1, are added together a thousand times. Each sum could be anything from 0 to 4, but most land near 2 - it takes four small numbers to make a sum near 0, and many different mixtures make one near 2. The histogram shows the pile in the middle: twenty bins from 0 to 4, redrawn five times a second.\n\nBottom: 1 1 2 2 2 3 10 in 3 bins with auto range. The range is 1 to 10, so the edges are 1 4 7 10: six values in the first bin, none in the middle, one - the 10 - in the last.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.percentile reads a single point off the same distribution. t.histc is the PyTorch version. np.rand makes evenly spread random values; adding several of them together is a quick bell curve.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
